#{[17592186287740 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287740 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181721" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287740 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287740 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,|5n|\\le 4}$\n\nII. $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,|5q|\\le 4}$\n\nIII. $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,|5x|\\le 4}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287740 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b4290422-629b-5741-89bd-a435f41aa909" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287740 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by solving the following inequality for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|5x|\\le 4 \\\\ 5|x|\\le 4 \\\\ |x|\\le \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4}{5}\\le x\\le \\frac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in I=[-\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{4}{5}].$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $|5n|\\le 4$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nA={0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $|5q|\\le 4$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊂C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287741 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287741 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181733" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287741 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287741 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,|4n|\\ge 8}$\n\nII. $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,|4q|\\ge 8}$\n\nIII. $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,|4x|\\ge 8}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287741 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "30977a65-8757-5cdf-8629-62e1df834a09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287741 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nWe start by solving the following inequality for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|4x|\\ge 8 \\\\ 4|x|\\ge 8 \\\\ |x|\\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nx\\le -2\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge 2\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in I=(-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $|4n|\\ge 8$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nA={…,-3,-2}\\cup {2,3,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $|4q|\\ge 8$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊂C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,B,$ and $C.$\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420305 #edb/ref 1080 "$C$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420305 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420305 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "099f3a38-4da4-5649-a22d-eba7684b0cfe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420306 #edb/ref 1080 "$B$ and $C$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420306 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420306 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1137ea51-0849-5c10-9f4b-2be034d8e893" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420307 #edb/ref 1080 "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420307 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420307 #edb/ref 