7592186420327 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420327 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420327 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420327 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420327 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186420315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420327 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "981d986a-6dfa-540d-944a-7be3c24f9b94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420328 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420328 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420328 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420328 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420328 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420328 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420328 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420328 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186420324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420328 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9ef252b3-0c31-53e5-9944-1acf64287b65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420329 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420329 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181669" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420329 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420329 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\pi )=0}$\n\nII. $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2q\\pi )=0}$\n\nIII. $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x\\pi )=0}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420329 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "787ecee8-f1f0-5525-9f42-a25a7295c8af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420329 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x\\pi )=0$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\pi  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+m\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{m}{2},\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There are no values of $m\\in Z$ such that $\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{m}{2}\\in Z.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nB={…,-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{4},…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊆C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420330 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420330 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181641" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420330 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420330 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{1-2x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n\nII. $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(3{e}^{x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n\nIII. $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(4{e}^{-x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420330 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a6b24cf2-2976-579e-8d0e-dd268f3c65a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420330 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{1-2x} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{1-2x})=-2{e}^{1-2x} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{e}^{1-2x})=4{e}^{1-2x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{e}^{1-2x})=-8{e}^{1-2x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nA={{e}^{1-2x},-2{e}^{1-2x},4{e}^{1-2x},-8{e}^{1-2x},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =3{e}^{x} \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x})=3{e}^{x} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x})=3{e}^{x} \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x})=3{e}^{x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $B$ contains only one element:\n\n$$\nB={3{e}^{x}}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{e}^{-x})=-4{e}^{-x} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4{e}^{-x})=4{e}^{-x} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{e}^{-x})=-4{e}^{-x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $C$ contains only two elements:\n\n$$\nC={4{e}^{-x},-4{e}^{-x}}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|C|=2,$ which is finite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420331 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420331 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181667" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420331 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420331 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2})=0}$\n\nII. $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{q\\pi }{2})=0}$\n\nIII. $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{2})=0}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420331 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c8147626-87d7-56ca-be09-d1e1490d794e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420331 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{2})=0$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x\\pi }{2} & =m\\pi  \\\\ x & =2m,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nA={…,-2,0,2,4,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Since $A$ is infinite, and $A⊆B,$ it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊆C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420332 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420332 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181706" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420332 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420332 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n\nII. $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(-\\sqrt{x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n\nIII. $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420332 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d8b98aed-4075-5dac-9091-d486170b806a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420332 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & =\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}})=\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}})=-\\frac{6}{25\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{8}}} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}},\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}},-\\frac{6}{25\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{8}}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $B$ is also infinite. Similar to the previous case, listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =-\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\sqrt{x})=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}} \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB={-\\sqrt{x},-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}},\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is also infinite. Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})=\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC={\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}},\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420580 #edb/ref 1080 "$A$ and $B$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420580 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420580 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4a84f9d9-81b1-54ae-8ae5-8c05ca06f33d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420581 #edb/ref 1080 "$A$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420581 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420581 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7a2e9409-26c5-5b50-9445-3a7e0cc1dff6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420582 #edb/ref 1080 "$A,B,$ and $C$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420582 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420582 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9a0ac365-22bb-5a1d-8ee4-097642b3a96a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420583 #edb/ref 1080 "$B$ and $C$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420583 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420583 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dd52b8a6-5559-526c-9e66-cb99de5e9630" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420584 #edb/ref 1080 "$A$ and $C$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420584 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420584 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f63bb298-46ab-531c-9644-243a96de9d51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420585 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420585 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420585 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420585 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420585 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420585 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420585 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420585 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186420584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420585 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0012b7e2-7b21-52ce-a8e6-93abfde08a7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420586 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420586 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181771" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420586 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420586 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,-{n}^{2}+n+12\\le 0}$\n\nII. $B={m\\in Z\\,:\\,-{m}^{2}+m+12\\ge 0}$\n\nIII. $C={q\\in Q\\,:\\,-{q}^{2}+q+12\\ge 0}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420586 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7a7b8c64-0e50-5904-90c5-c8b0f74761de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420586 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y=-{x}^{2}+x+12$ for $x\\in R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+x+12 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-x-12) & =0 \\\\ -(x-4)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-3$ and $x=4,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/57/57d58b579557ba0ea8c6179bd37209c8199eee83e17d79c74828a051aa1382d6.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality $-{x}^{2}+x+12\\le 0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\nx\\le -3\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge 4\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{1}=(-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [4,\\infty ).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality $-{x}^{2}+x+12\\ge 0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\n-3\\le x\\le 4\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{2}=[-3,4].$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $-{n}^{2}+n+12\\le 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={…,-4,-3}\\cup {4,5,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. If $m\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $-{m}^{2}+m+12\\ge 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=8,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $-{q}^{2}+q+12\\ge 0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
